WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.943 --> 00:00:06.110
COLMAN MADDEN: Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:07.510
My name is Colman
Madden, and I'm

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:09.880
an engineer with Google
Cloud in New York focused

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.450
on asset management in
the hedge fund industry.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:15.010
Prior to Google, I spent
around 10 years working

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:16.900
in the data engineering space.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:18.940
I'm also here with
David Li from BlackRock,

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:22.750
whom I've been collaborating
with since early 2019.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:24.830
David, over to you.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:26.100
DAVID LI: Thank you, Colman.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.420
Hi, my name is David.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.530
I work in the financial
modeling group at BlackRock.

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.970
The group consists of a
team of engineers, modelers,

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.430
and researchers working
together to develop best

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.700
in class analytics to clients.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.670
Today's agenda is as follows.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.020
I'll first introduce
what we do at BlackRock,

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.000
then present two
use cases of how

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.770
we generate signals
using natural language

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.080
processing on Google Cloud.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.210
First, I'll talk a
little bit about how

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:55.020
we train against returns to
construct a bespoke dictionary

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.390
to analyze sentiment
for single issuers

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.350
in the structured credit space.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:01.600
Second, I'll talk about
knowledge graphs and entity

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.070
linking, and about how we
trained an entity linking model

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:08.578
to build a foundation for
constructing knowledge graphs.

00:01:08.578 --> 00:01:10.120
In addition, I'll
talk about some use

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.100
cases for these concepts.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.370
Colman will then conclude
with forward-looking views

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.657
of how to conduct investment
research on Google

00:01:17.657 --> 00:01:18.490
Cloud in the future.

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:25.440
I lead a team inside FMG
called advanced data analytics.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.990
Our responsibilities
span engineering,

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.570
financial modeling, and
natural language processing.

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:32.590
For data engineering,
we process data

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:35.640
sets that are terabytes
in size to extract insight

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.820
by leveraging big data
technologies such as Spark.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.220
Some of our work have
been previously presented

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.445
at Spark Summit East.

00:01:43.445 --> 00:01:44.820
We also leverage
managed services

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.980
provided by public clouds.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:50.310
Our goal is to efficiently
deliver data driven solutions

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.870
to our clients.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:54.390
As a team, we have a
wide range of skills

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:57.360
and provide flexible, end to
end solutions to streamline

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.910
future engineering, the
building of financial models,

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:01.920
and productionization.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.800
Our approach to natural language
processing is a little bit more

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.520
focused on research.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.520
We pay a lot of attention
to recent developments

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.520
in the applications of
text classification,

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.610
information extraction, and
entity linking in an NLP space.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.800
The first use case
is classifying text

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.880
in a structured credit market.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.670
In the past decade or so, the
leveraged loans market size

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:26.730
has been increasing.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:30.330
Also, due to private nature
of these investments, publicly

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:35.240
available structured data sets
may be common, but sparse.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.830
Taking a bottoms-up
approach, we analyze credit

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.170
at the issuer level.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.480
We aim to tackle the problem
with future engineering

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.520
to add more insight
to the model, which

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.687
is to look at issuer defaults
and measure probability

00:02:47.687 --> 00:02:48.770
of default from an issuer.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:55.760
At the beginning of the year,
the US leveraged loans market

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.100
was around $1.2 trillion.

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:01.280
More than half of that is the US
collateralized loan obligations

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.190
market, which is
close to $700 billion.

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:05.870
In the past two
years, we've seen

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:09.680
record issuances of close
to $120 billion per year

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.110
in 2018 and 2019.

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:14.810
This increased
market size, coupled

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:16.700
with companies
assuming more debt,

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.920
give rise to additional
risk in the market.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.130
At the same time, risks are
becoming increasingly difficult

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:24.300
to capture.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.150
For example, environmental
changes such as COVID

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.760
had significant impacts to
the competitive landscape

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.870
in multiple sectors
such as retail.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:35.420
As the political landscape and
regulatory oversight changes

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:39.470
over time, companies are exposed
to increased uncertainty.

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:42.920
Additionally, they're also
held to higher standards.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:45.700
Companies are now
facing mounting pressure

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:49.130
on environmental,
social, and governance.

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:50.990
In order to address
these issues and capture

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:53.570
additional information
from alternative data sets,

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:55.770
we turn to natural
language processing.

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:58.760
We analyze large volumes
of textual news via NLP

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:01.030
to classify text
into sentiment scores

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:03.995
as well as other metrics such
as news volume and emotions

00:04:03.995 --> 00:04:04.745
in these articles.

00:04:08.688 --> 00:04:10.980
Some of the challenges in
predicting issuer probability

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:14.600
of default, or PD, over
some time horizon are,

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:17.910
first, it's typically difficult
to find structured data

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:20.173
on issuers due to
their private nature.

00:04:20.173 --> 00:04:23.150
In these instances of where
data is actually available,

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:25.150
it can still be
subject to sparcity,

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:28.440
such as pricing data, due
to these instruments lacking

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:29.950
in liquidity.

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:32.600
Trying to apply NLP
to this problem,

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.360
available news
corpora are typically

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:37.900
at least terabytes in size,
and can easily exceed petabytes

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:40.120
across multiple periods.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:43.870
Given these, we need both
persistence and compute

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:47.680
resources to be flexible,
whereas they typically

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:51.160
are constrained in a
fixed computation farm.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:52.870
Thus we need a
flexible environment

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:56.080
to iterate and test our ideas
so we can extract and classify

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:00.740
text for predicting
probability of default.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.770
Let's take a look at the
modeling process here.

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:06.170
First, we start off
with loading the data.

00:05:06.170 --> 00:05:09.287
Then we apply transformations
such as tokenization stemming,

00:05:09.287 --> 00:05:11.120
named entity recognition,
and sometimes even

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:12.710
applying language
models to reason

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:14.970
about text in the
embedding space.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.660
Then we align unstructured
text with structured data sets

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:20.060
such as returns or spreads.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:21.770
This step has to
be done carefully

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:24.440
to match market open
and close times as well

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:27.500
as time zones, and ways to
deduplicate articles that

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:29.790
discuss the exact same thing.

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:33.440
Once this is done, we query
the data stored and train

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:36.050
and evaluate our ideas by
tuning our hyperparameters

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:37.700
and finding thresholds
to construct

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:40.320
an optimal bespoke
sentiment dictionary.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.280
Then we intuitively
roll this dictionary

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:45.035
across time for inference.

00:05:47.678 --> 00:05:50.220
This process has proven to be
quite straightforward on Google

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:51.340
Cloud.

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:53.670
We start off by persisting
various stages of our data

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:56.580
to Cloud Storage, which is a
highly available distributed

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:58.440
store that gives us
plenty of scratch areas

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:00.330
to test our ideas.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:03.590
Then we provision Dataproc
clusters with GCloud

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:05.030
in Cloud Console.

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:08.360
This provides us with an
ephemeral Hadoop Spark compute

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:11.300
cluster across our
large data sets.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:13.460
An auto-scaling policy
and preemptible machines

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:16.360
are your best friend when
managing these clusters.

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:18.820
Recent metrics demonstrate
that Spark 3 performance

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:21.013
on Kubernetes is
catching up to YARN.

00:06:21.013 --> 00:06:22.930
This is good news as we
could then potentially

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:26.050
switch to Spark
operators in the future.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:29.470
After that, we leverage BigQuery
to run analytical workloads

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:32.950
and extract and filter
relevant information.

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:35.650
BigQuery has similar
functions as Hive,

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:38.170
storing metadata on
[INAUDIBLE] Compute Engine.

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:40.610
However for BigQuery, there's
no tuning or configuration

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:41.480
involved.

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:43.190
It automagically
handles your workloads

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:45.520
and produces results quickly.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:48.880
Finally, we use AI
notebooks and Cloud Storage

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:51.910
as a combination to iterate
over our training and evaluation

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:52.960
cycle.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:55.090
These managed notebooks
give us the flexibility

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:57.500
to scale our resources up and
down through configuration

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:58.000
changes.

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:03.820
Usually operating
on premise, we need

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:06.430
to be very careful about
resource consumption as well

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:08.800
as capacity planning due
to the multi-tenancy nature

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:11.080
of our shared compute clusters.

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:14.020
Typically, a single ETF job
that we've previously done

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:16.150
can take up to about an
hour when we have thousands

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:18.000
of these jobs running.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:21.340
On Google Cloud, after
carefully configuring our setup,

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:23.800
we're able to compact
that time per iteration

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:26.870
down to two hours for
the entire data set.

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:29.400
Armed with scalability
and flexibility

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:32.370
to change the cluster size as
well as the type of cluster,

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:34.290
we can iterate on
multiple tests and focus

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:36.743
more of our time on
ideation and modeling

00:07:36.743 --> 00:07:38.910
as opposed to worrying about
whether we're consuming

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:41.430
too much compute resources.

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:43.990
Some lessons we learned
during this exercise--

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:47.400
Spark, the de facto open source
big data processing tool,

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:50.610
is great for ETL on
disk I/O intensive jobs.

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:53.360
For large-scale
optimization calculations--

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:54.690
not so much.

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:57.510
We turn to distributed compute
frameworks such as Dask and Ray

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:59.790
for parallelizing these
optimization tasks

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:01.275
to optimize our runtime--

00:08:01.275 --> 00:08:02.400
or to minimize our runtime.

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:08.447
Let's switch gears
to our second topic.

00:08:08.447 --> 00:08:10.030
This one is more
exploratory in nature

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:12.970
and has received increased
attention in the past few years

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:14.420
in the research community.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:17.290
We explore the applications of
entity linking and knowledge

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:19.600
graphs in the equity space.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:23.750
We want to extract information
from the correct securities.

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:25.550
Moreover, we want to
track and identify

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:27.800
relationships between entities.

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:29.915
So we need entity
linking to map text

00:08:29.915 --> 00:08:31.415
to an entity in
some knowledge base.

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:39.400
Some examples of applications
are extracting and analyzing

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:41.559
supply chain impacts
to companies, revenues,

00:08:41.559 --> 00:08:43.030
and expenses.

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:46.820
Think of Foxconn
manufacturing iPhones.

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:51.500
Additionally, Samsung is a
supplier of screens for Apple.

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:53.390
Therefore, understanding
correlation

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:56.110
of performance between companies
due to their sheer dependencies

00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:58.310
on the same suppliers
help us better

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:01.130
build equity risk models.

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:03.110
Similarly, we can analyze
corporate structure

00:09:03.110 --> 00:09:04.780
such as ownership
in subsidiaries.

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:08.770
And here we define
ownership kind of loosely.

00:09:08.770 --> 00:09:12.160
We analyze this to gain insight
how performance of one company

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:14.980
affects another.

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:18.070
Lastly, we were talking
about ESG before.

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:20.800
Creating models and measuring
corporate governance can

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:24.700
be aided with calculating the
diversity of boards, experience

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:27.760
and expertise of board members,
and how a member's track

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:31.951
record may affect the company
performance that they sit on.

00:09:31.951 --> 00:09:34.440
On the right-hand side,
you can see a snapshot

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:36.340
of a personal knowledge graph.

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:39.852
It shows the ownership relations
of BlackRock as well as Google.

00:09:39.852 --> 00:09:41.310
And the two companies
are connected

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:44.190
through Alphabet, which is
the parent company of Google,

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:45.855
and in which
BlackRock owns shares.

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:53.100
To construct a knowledge
graph, a fundamental step

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:57.370
is to look at the problem
of entity linking or EL.

00:09:57.370 --> 00:10:00.760
Here's a simple example
given a sentence,

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:03.862
"Larry, whom we as the
chairman and CEO of BlackRock,

00:10:03.862 --> 00:10:05.320
said, at the
beginning of the year,

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:07.870
that BlackRock would
place sustainability

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:10.860
at the center of the investment
approach at the firm."

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:13.020
In this example, it's
relatively easy for humans

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:15.555
to understand that "Larry"
refers to Laurence T. Fink, who

00:10:15.555 --> 00:10:17.160
is the CEO of BlackRock.

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:20.240
And BlackRock is the
investment management company.

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:23.120
For a model, the difficulty
lies in training a system that

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:25.550
can correctly link
text bands to entities

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:29.030
that live in some knowledge
base such as Wikipedia.

00:10:29.030 --> 00:10:31.220
The objective of
ELs is as following.

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:34.010
Give it a piece of text
recognized as [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:34.010 --> 00:10:37.790
and link entities to an entry
defined in some knowledge base.

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:39.620
We use BERT as our
language model--

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:42.710
BERT was developed by
Google AI in 2018--

00:10:42.710 --> 00:10:46.160
and a knowledge base that's a
mix of Wikipedia other sources.

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:48.640
In this case, we use Iago.

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:52.690
Our task is to detect mentions
and disambiguate entities.

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:55.250
Looking at this problem, we
need to provision resources

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:57.280
that can scale
with our workload,

00:10:57.280 --> 00:10:59.290
namely, deliver us
GPUs for training

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:01.200
of large neural networks.

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:03.490
The productionization
process of these models

00:11:03.490 --> 00:11:05.800
have typically been complex.

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:08.440
And we show how we can use
Google Cloud to achieve this.

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:15.750
Looking at the following
high-level architecture,

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:18.750
we can see that connecting
BlackRock and Google

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:20.700
networks through
interconnect and keeping

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:24.870
the traffic between the data
centers is a good way to start.

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:27.690
With a remote interpreter on
a virtual machine instance

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:29.760
on Compute Engine,
we're able to test

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:32.600
different types of
configurations of GPUs and TPUs

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:34.640
and tune them for training.

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:36.650
We then set up a
request server to run

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:41.270
evaluation on a local machine,
and then forward the port.

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:44.390
Lastly, we deploy our models
through TensorFlow serving

00:11:44.390 --> 00:11:48.060
to expose the entity linking
model to clients through an API

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:50.060
through cloud endpoints.

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:51.680
This end to end
approach allowed us

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:54.110
to quickly iterate,
and train our models,

00:11:54.110 --> 00:11:57.510
and minimize the deployment time
of our models to production.

00:11:57.510 --> 00:11:59.280
Next, I'll hand
it over to Colman

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:01.980
to give a forward-looking
view of investment research

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:02.752
at Google Cloud.

00:12:02.752 --> 00:12:03.960
COLMAN MADDEN: Thanks, David.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:06.630
So David covered
two great use cases

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:09.457
where his team has been
leveraging GCP at BlackRock.

00:12:09.457 --> 00:12:11.040
And I wanted to talk
a little bit more

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:13.152
broadly on how the
investment industry is

00:12:13.152 --> 00:12:14.360
partnering with Google Cloud.

00:12:17.762 --> 00:12:20.220
I don't think it's any secret
that the buy side is becoming

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:22.480
increasingly dependent
on data and AI

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:24.247
to power investment decisions.

00:12:24.247 --> 00:12:26.580
And you'll see that by looking
at some of the statistics

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:28.800
here on the right-hand
side of the slide.

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:30.900
At Google Cloud, we
believe this aligns

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:33.900
really well with our powerful
analytics platform and the AI

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:35.730
research coming out
of Google Brain,

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:37.290
which we provide
to our customers

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:38.415
through our Cloud platform.

00:12:41.170 --> 00:12:44.090
Why don't we start by looking
at a typical investment decision

00:12:44.090 --> 00:12:46.430
flow for a buy side firm.

00:12:46.430 --> 00:12:48.820
So firstly, formulate
an investment idea.

00:12:48.820 --> 00:12:51.640
Source data to prove
out that hypothesis.

00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:53.630
Generate signals from the data.

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:55.170
Construct the portfolio.

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:56.730
Backtest the portfolio.

00:12:56.730 --> 00:12:57.700
Present the findings.

00:12:57.700 --> 00:12:59.200
And determine a
capital allocation

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:03.490
from an investment committee
before moving into production.

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:06.310
The blue box highlights
the stages of process

00:13:06.310 --> 00:13:08.110
we feel are a great
fit for Google Cloud.

00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:15.900
So why run investment
research on Google Cloud?

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:18.960
Firstly, to provide the ability
for investing teams to spin up

00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:21.150
projects quickly
and react to events

00:13:21.150 --> 00:13:23.310
in the market in
an agile fashion.

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:25.920
Two, streamline the
onboarding of new data sets

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:29.430
from commercial data vendors,
such as Refinitiv and CME Group

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:31.350
through our data marketplace.

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:33.840
Three, burst compute
workloads into the cloud,

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:35.670
and eliminate
resource contention

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:38.360
from on-prem compute firms.

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:41.130
Four, get access to the latest
AI research through our APIs

00:13:41.130 --> 00:13:42.310
and managed services.

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:45.330
And five, orchestrate your ML
workflows with state-of-the-art

00:13:45.330 --> 00:13:49.470
DevOps and MLOps platform.

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:51.780
So how do we do all
this on Google Cloud?

00:13:55.423 --> 00:13:56.840
Here's the high-level
architecture

00:13:56.840 --> 00:14:00.260
of how firms are running an
investment research on GCP.

00:14:00.260 --> 00:14:02.180
We start off by looking
at the blue box.

00:14:02.180 --> 00:14:04.230
This is our data
acquisition project

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:05.960
where the data
engineering teams onboard

00:14:05.960 --> 00:14:08.810
various data sets such
as market, portfolio,

00:14:08.810 --> 00:14:10.820
news data, et cetera.

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:13.130
And if we look at the green
and yellow boxes below,

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:15.380
this is where the research
teams are using their tools

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:17.630
to access the data sets above.

00:14:17.630 --> 00:14:19.610
We understand
there's often a wall

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:21.488
between different
investment teams.

00:14:21.488 --> 00:14:23.780
And we can implement this
using our identity and access

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:26.540
management tools while
still allowing access

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:29.900
to shared resources
such as ticket stream.

00:14:29.900 --> 00:14:31.810
So let's dive into
the GCP products

00:14:31.810 --> 00:14:34.540
that sit behind these boxes.

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:36.520
In the data
acquisition project, we

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:39.445
can ingest streaming data
via our PubSub message queue,

00:14:39.445 --> 00:14:41.830
or land files in
Google Cloud Storage,

00:14:41.830 --> 00:14:46.030
trigger load jobs into BigQuery
or NoOps data warehouse.

00:14:46.030 --> 00:14:48.550
Dataflow and Dataproc
are available for ETL.

00:14:48.550 --> 00:14:51.130
We're also seeing a lot of
customers moving to an ELT

00:14:51.130 --> 00:14:54.188
architecture where they load
the data as quickly as possible

00:14:54.188 --> 00:14:56.230
and then use the power of
BigQuery to actually do

00:14:56.230 --> 00:14:58.832
that transformation
using AntiSQL.

00:14:58.832 --> 00:15:01.510
The data marketplace
allows data publishers,

00:15:01.510 --> 00:15:04.562
aggregators to share their
data sets with financial firms

00:15:04.562 --> 00:15:05.770
without the need for any ETL.

00:15:05.770 --> 00:15:08.920
This is extremely valuable,
as data sets are often

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:11.200
in the petabytes.

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:13.960
You may have seen we just
announced BigQuery Omni, which

00:15:13.960 --> 00:15:18.730
will enable firms to query
the data on AWS or Azure

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:21.430
from a single interface without
any cross-cloud movement

00:15:21.430 --> 00:15:22.930
of our copies of data.

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:26.220
We push the compute to the data.

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:29.110
In the research projects,
quantitative researchers

00:15:29.110 --> 00:15:31.390
can run simulations
on HPC clusters

00:15:31.390 --> 00:15:34.150
with hundreds of thousands
of cores, custom VMs

00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:36.790
that perfect the ratio
of cores to memory.

00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:38.890
If your workflow can handle
preemptions and needs

00:15:38.890 --> 00:15:41.550
to run for less than 24
hours, preemptible VMs

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:44.480
allow you to do this
at an 80% discount.

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:46.230
We just launched
confidential VMs,

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:49.540
which allow you to encrypt
data during processing,

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:52.390
and even when it's in memory.

00:15:52.390 --> 00:15:55.870
So moving to the right,
our Cloud AI platform

00:15:55.870 --> 00:15:58.870
allows quantitative researchers
to create AI notebooks

00:15:58.870 --> 00:16:00.760
with the latest
TensorFlow, PyTorch,

00:16:00.760 --> 00:16:04.480
and scikit-learn images
on CPUs, GPUs, or TPUs,

00:16:04.480 --> 00:16:07.210
and pull this data--
these data sets straight

00:16:07.210 --> 00:16:10.490
into Pandas DataFrames.

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:12.950
AutoML allows you to create
machine learning models

00:16:12.950 --> 00:16:15.740
for natural language, structured
data translation without having

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:17.002
to write any code.

00:16:17.002 --> 00:16:18.710
These models can be
trained to understand

00:16:18.710 --> 00:16:21.500
terminology that is specific
to the financial services

00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:22.640
industry.

00:16:22.640 --> 00:16:24.710
Our Document AI
solutions enable you

00:16:24.710 --> 00:16:27.170
to process complex financial
filings and earnings

00:16:27.170 --> 00:16:29.900
reports with cutting-edge
NLP and transfer

00:16:29.900 --> 00:16:32.010
learning technologies.

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:35.600
AI pipelines is built on
top of Kubeflow and TFX

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:40.660
and provides an MLOps solution
to evaluate, train, and deploy

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:42.980
machine learning
models to production.

00:16:42.980 --> 00:16:45.850
These models can be shared
with other research teams

00:16:45.850 --> 00:16:48.253
through AI Hub.

00:16:48.253 --> 00:16:49.670
One really important
thing to note

00:16:49.670 --> 00:16:52.220
here is that this is all
deployed by infrastructure

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:56.340
as code using something like
deployment manager or terraform

00:16:56.340 --> 00:16:56.840
scripts.

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:03.190
So how do you get started?

00:17:03.190 --> 00:17:07.140
So step one, you want to get
the cloud foundations in place.

00:17:07.140 --> 00:17:10.380
Understand your requirements
around networking, security,

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:11.640
access management.

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:14.910
Wrap these up in
infrastructure as code.

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:17.859
And feed these into your
project creation pipeline.

00:17:17.859 --> 00:17:21.460
Secondly, you want to understand
the process for onboarding

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:23.829
new data sets and build
a data acquisition

00:17:23.829 --> 00:17:25.960
pipeline to automate this.

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:28.580
Thirdly, understand what
tools your researchers need,

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:30.860
and automate the
deployment of these tools,

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:32.620
along with the
connectivity they need

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:35.470
to access the data required
to run their experiments.

00:17:35.470 --> 00:17:38.240
We want to make life
easier for everyone.

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:40.280
Thank you for coming
along to our talk today.

00:17:40.280 --> 00:17:41.890
Please add any
questions to Dory.

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:45.090
Or feel free to reach out
to me or David directly.

